---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { elementCount } from '../../data/site-stats';

const headings = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'what-it-fixes', label: 'What it fixes' },
  { id: 'usage', label: 'Rendering and hydrating' },
  { id: 'fouc', label: 'The FOUC guard' },
  { id: 'coverage', label: 'Component coverage' },
  { id: 'routers', label: 'Client-side routers' },
  { id: 'frameworks', label: 'Framework support' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Server rendering"
  description="Server-render ARC UI components to declarative shadow DOM, and hydrate them without a flash."
  activeSlug="server-rendering"
  headings={headings}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Server rendering' }]}
>
  <div class="prose-page">
    <h1>Server rendering</h1>

    <section id="overview">
      <p>
        ARC UI components server-render through <code>@lit-labs/ssr</code> into
        <strong>declarative shadow DOM</strong>: a <code>&lt;template shadowrootmode="open"&gt;</code>
        the browser turns into a real shadow root while parsing, before any JavaScript runs.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>All {elementCount} custom elements</strong> (components and their child items)
        render this way. Server rendering is optional. A page with no build step, or one loading
        ARC UI from a CDN, works without it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="what-it-fixes">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">What it fixes</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p>
        Many ARC UI components take their content from attributes, not slots. Before JavaScript
        loads, or without it, a tag like this one is empty. There's no heading or description,
        and nothing for a crawler to read.
      </p>
      <arc-code-block language="html" code={`<arc-feature-card
  heading="Fast by default"
  description="Ships 40% less JavaScript"
></arc-feature-card>`}></arc-code-block>
      <p>
        Here is the same tag after the server renderer, with Lit's hydration markers left out.
        The content is in the HTML response:
      </p>
      <arc-code-block language="html" code={`<arc-feature-card
  heading="Fast by default"
  description="Ships 40% less JavaScript"
>
  <template shadowrootmode="open">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/_arc/s-y0rti11lg82db.css">
    <div class="card" part="card">
      <div class="card__inner" part="inner">
        <h3 class="card__title" part="title">
          Fast by default
        </h3>
        <p class="card__desc" part="description">
          Ships 40% less JavaScript
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </template>
</arc-feature-card>`}></arc-code-block>
      <p>
        <strong>71 of the {elementCount} elements</strong> have no slots at all, so without
        server rendering their content isn't in the HTML. Components that take their content
        from slots already have it in the page, and gain less.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="usage">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Rendering and hydrating</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p>On the server, render as you would any Lit template:</p>
      <arc-code-block language="js" code={`import { render } from '@lit-labs/ssr';
import { collectResult } from '@lit-labs/ssr/lib/render-result.js';
import { html } from 'lit';
import '@arclux/arc-ui/feature-card';

const markup = await collectResult(render(html\`
  <arc-feature-card heading="Fast by default"></arc-feature-card>
\`));`}></arc-code-block>

      <p>
        On the client, import the hydration entry <strong>before any component</strong>:
      </p>
      <arc-code-block language="js" code={`import '@arclux/arc-ui/hydrate';   // first — order matters
import '@arclux/arc-ui/register';`}></arc-code-block>

      <p>
        Without it, when an element upgrades, Lit renders its template from scratch into the
        shadow root the server already filled. That throws away identical DOM and causes the
        flash server rendering is meant to prevent. With it, Lit <em>adopts</em> the existing
        nodes and renders nothing. The order matters because hydration support patches
        <code>LitElement</code>'s update path, and a component class defined before the patch
        never gets it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="fouc">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">The FOUC guard</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p>
        <code>base.css</code> hides ARC UI elements until they upgrade, so a page doesn't flash
        unstyled custom elements. A server-rendered element hasn't upgraded yet but is already
        <em>finished</em>, so that rule would hide its content until hydration. Server rendering
        would then give you a longer blank screen instead of a shorter one.
      </p>
      <p>Opt the page out at the root:</p>
      <arc-code-block language="html" code={`<html data-arc-ssr>`}></arc-code-block>
      <p>
        You have to set it yourself because CSS can't detect a declarative shadow root. The
        browser consumes the <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> during parsing and leaves nothing a
        selector can match.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="coverage">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Component coverage</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p>
        <code>pnpm check ssr</code> renders every element in Node and fails on any that throws.
        It runs in CI. On the server, Lit runs the constructor, <code>willUpdate</code>
        and <code>render</code>, and none of <code>connectedCallback</code>,
        <code>firstUpdated</code>, <code>updated</code>, or a reactive controller's
        <code>hostConnected</code>. Browser work belongs in those.
      </p>
      <p>
        All {elementCount} pass, so none are client-only, and you opt in per page.
      </p>
      <p>
        A second test checks that every component's docs preview, server-rendered, looks the
        same before its script arrives as after. That includes components built from their
        children: breadcrumbs, navigation menus, tabs, accordions and lists arrive with their
        items, and nothing moves when the script loads. The two exceptions depend on the
        current time: <code>arc-clock</code> shows it, and <code>arc-activity-heatmap</code>
        without an <code>end-date</code> ends today. Only the browser knows either.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="routers">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Client-side routers</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p>
        A router that fetches the next page and parses it with <code>DOMParser</code> gets
        declarative shadow roots as inert templates: only the page parser attaches them. Astro's
        <code>&lt;ClientRouter /&gt;</code> is one. Attach them before the swap:
      </p>
      <arc-code-block language="js" code={`import { attachShadowRoots } from '@arclux/arc-ui/shadow-roots';

document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', (e) => attachShadowRoots(e.newDocument));`}></arc-code-block>
      <p>
        Without it, every component on the incoming page renders a fresh copy next to an inert
        template, and nothing hydrates.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="frameworks">
      <h2 class="docs-heading">Framework support</h2>
      <arc-divider align="left" variant="line-gradient" style="max-width:48px;margin-bottom:var(--space-md)"></arc-divider>
      <p>
        Lit's framework SSR integrations work from the framework's component graph, and React
        is the only framework with one. <code>@arclux/arc-ui/ssr</code> doesn't need the graph.
        A framework's server render produces HTML, and every <code>&lt;arc-*&gt;</code> in it
        can be rendered to a declarative shadow root from the markup alone. So it takes HTML
        and returns HTML, whatever produced it.
      </p>
      <arc-code-block language="js" code={`import { renderDeclarativeShadowDOM } from '@arclux/arc-ui/ssr';

const { html, stylesheets } = await renderDeclarativeShadowDOM(pageHtml);
// serve html; write each stylesheet under /_arc`}></arc-code-block>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nuxt, SvelteKit, Angular Universal, Next, Astro, or a string you built by
          hand</strong>: pipe the rendered HTML through it. This site's Astro build runs every
          page through it. The others have no test or example in this repo.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>React</strong>: <code>@lit-labs/ssr-react</code> also works, if you would rather
          render through the component graph.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        It renders any Lit element defined in the process, including other libraries'. Import
        their registration before rendering (<code>@arclux/brand/register</code>, your own
        components) and their elements are rendered too.
      </p>
      <p>
        It needs <code>@lit-labs/ssr</code>, an optional peer dependency. Install it in the
        project that server-renders. Two things are up to you: serve the returned
        stylesheets from the path they were linked with, and import
        <code>@arclux/arc-ui/hydrate</code> on the client <em>before any component is
        defined</em>. On a bundler that usually means forcing it into its own chunk, since an
        import statement alone does not control evaluation order.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .prose-page { max-width: 70ch; }
  .prose-page h1 { font-family: var(--font-display); margin-bottom: var(--space-lg); }
  .prose-page h2 { margin-top: var(--space-2xl); }
  .prose-page p { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
  .prose-page ul { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7; padding-inline-start: var(--space-lg); margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
  .prose-page li { margin-bottom: var(--space-sm); }
  .prose-page pre {
    background: var(--surface-raised);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: var(--space-md);
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }
  .prose-page code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--_text-sm); }
  .prose-page strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
</style>
